            <ArticleTitle>A Mitochondrial Disorder in a Middle Age Iranian Patient: Report of a Rare Case.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>337-341</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.18869/nirp.bcn.8.4.337</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="Introduction" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes (MELAS) can involve multiple systems and cause stroke-like episodes and status epilepticus.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Case Presentation" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">A 48-year-old female with history of early fatigability, migraine-type headaches, and bilateral sensory-neural hearing loss presented 3 episodes of serial seizures. On admission she was affected by Wernicke aphasia and, then, right hemiparesis. Investigations showed elevated arterial lactate and ragged red fibers on muscle biopsy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Conclusion" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Though more commonly diagnosed during childhood, some cases of adult-onset MELAS syndrome are reported. This syndrome should be considered in patients with stroke-like events in adults without cerebrovascular risk factors and difficult-to-treat seizures.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
